WEBVTT

00:03.800 --> 00:06.400
All right, moving on to the next use case for the case statements.

00:06.400 --> 00:08.480
We can use it in order to handle nulls.

00:08.480 --> 00:11.600
Handling nulls means replace a null with a value.

00:11.600 --> 00:17.280
And as we learned before with the window aggregate functions, sometimes nulls leads to incorrect calculations

00:17.320 --> 00:20.360
and results, which leads to wrong decision making.

00:20.360 --> 00:24.560
We're going to have later a dedicated chapter on how to handle nulls in SQL, but now we're going to

00:24.560 --> 00:27.440
learn how to handle nulls using case statements.

00:27.440 --> 00:28.680
So now let's have the following task.

00:28.680 --> 00:35.320
And it says find the average scores of customers and treat nulls as zero and additionally provide details

00:35.320 --> 00:37.400
such as customer ID and the last name.

00:37.440 --> 00:39.240
Okay, so now let's solve it step by step.

00:39.400 --> 00:41.080
And again we have here details.

00:41.080 --> 00:42.520
And as well we have to do aggregations.

00:42.520 --> 00:45.200
That means we have to go and use the window functions.

00:45.200 --> 00:47.480
And we don't have to forget that we have to treat the nulls.

00:47.480 --> 00:48.600
So we have to handle it.

00:48.800 --> 00:51.760
So now let's go and start with very simple select.

00:51.760 --> 00:57.000
So select customer ID we need the last name.

00:57.440 --> 00:59.120
And as well we need the scores.

00:59.600 --> 01:04.680
So from sales customers let's go and execute it.

01:04.730 --> 01:07.130
So as usual, we have our five customers and the scores.

01:07.170 --> 01:08.210
And here we have a null.

01:08.250 --> 01:12.490
Now we're going to go and write the window function but without handling the nulls just in order to

01:12.490 --> 01:13.530
see the differences.

01:13.530 --> 01:15.810
So we need the average function.

01:16.010 --> 01:16.610
For what.

01:16.650 --> 01:20.050
For the scores do we have to now partition the data.

01:20.250 --> 01:20.850
Well no.

01:20.850 --> 01:22.170
So we're going to leave it as empty.

01:22.210 --> 01:24.850
We need the average score of all customers.

01:24.850 --> 01:25.650
So that's it.

01:25.690 --> 01:29.010
Let's go and give it a name and then execute it.

01:29.010 --> 01:30.170
I think I have here a mistake.

01:30.170 --> 01:31.930
So it is a score not scores.

01:31.930 --> 01:35.530
So and now as you can see we have the average of 625.

01:35.570 --> 01:40.570
And as we learned before SQL can go and summarize all those four values and divide it by four.

01:40.610 --> 01:45.050
But our business understands that nulls as zero not as missing information.

01:45.050 --> 01:47.050
So we have to go and handle the null.

01:47.170 --> 01:50.690
Let's go and create a new column for the scores.

01:51.010 --> 01:54.010
But this time we're going to go and use the case statements.

01:54.010 --> 01:55.090
It's going to be very simple.

01:55.090 --> 01:59.890
So we're going to say when the score is null.

02:00.130 --> 02:03.850
So in SQL we don't write equal null we say is null.

02:03.850 --> 02:07.050
So with that we are replacing the nulls with zero right.

02:07.410 --> 02:09.830
So now otherwise what can happen?

02:09.830 --> 02:10.990
So if it's not null.

02:10.990 --> 02:14.150
So we need the score as it is, we should not manipulate anything.

02:14.390 --> 02:17.230
So the default value is the score itself.

02:17.230 --> 02:18.590
If the score is not null.

02:18.830 --> 02:23.790
So now let's go and end it and let's call it score clean.

02:24.190 --> 02:26.390
So let's go and execute it.

02:26.390 --> 02:30.270
Now if you check the result over here it's like almost identical as the score.

02:30.430 --> 02:32.830
So we don't have any new values for the scores.

02:32.830 --> 02:35.110
But only the nulls now are zero.

02:35.110 --> 02:37.510
And all other values they are not affected.

02:37.510 --> 02:38.470
So we didn't touch it.

02:38.510 --> 02:39.990
We didn't transform it at all.

02:39.990 --> 02:44.310
So this is what do we mean with handling nulls replacing nulls with another value.

02:44.310 --> 02:49.550
So now in order to finish the task, we have to do the average for the score clean and not for the original

02:49.550 --> 02:49.990
score.

02:49.990 --> 02:50.790
So how are we going to do it?

02:50.790 --> 02:53.190
Let's go and copy the whole case statements.

02:53.190 --> 02:55.470
I'm just going to do it in another column.

02:55.470 --> 02:57.110
So let's have an average.

02:57.350 --> 03:00.510
And inside it we have the case statements like this.

03:00.870 --> 03:03.670
Let me just sort it like this.

03:03.670 --> 03:07.590
And now what is missing is the over and it's going to be empty.

03:07.990 --> 03:11.310
So average customer let's call it clean.

03:11.430 --> 03:13.000
So this is the logic.

03:13.400 --> 03:15.240
Let me just make everything smaller.

03:15.680 --> 03:18.200
So as you can see, it's exactly like the previous one.

03:18.200 --> 03:23.880
But instead of using the original score, now we are using the column that we have created.

03:23.880 --> 03:26.120
But of course we don't need the alias over here.

03:26.320 --> 03:27.480
So we have to remove it.

03:27.480 --> 03:29.480
So it starts with case and end.

03:29.520 --> 03:31.280
So let's go and execute it.

03:31.280 --> 03:35.400
And now you can see in the output we got a new value for the average.

03:35.400 --> 03:37.640
And it is more accurate for the business.

03:37.640 --> 03:38.760
So now we have 500.

03:38.800 --> 03:41.240
Previously we had 625.

03:41.240 --> 03:46.240
So as you can see you have to understand what the nulls means in your business and handle it correctly.

03:46.240 --> 03:48.080
Otherwise you will get wrong results.

03:48.120 --> 03:48.760
So that's it.

03:48.800 --> 03:52.400
We use case statements in order to handle the nulls inside our data.

03:56.280 --> 04:02.080
Conditional aggregations means we can go and apply an aggregate function in SQL like sum average count,

04:02.120 --> 04:07.600
but this time only on a subset of data that meet specific conditions.

04:07.600 --> 04:13.520
This technique is amazing in order to do deep dive analysis or target analysis on a specific subset

04:13.560 --> 04:14.200
of the data.

04:14.240 --> 04:19.250
So now let's have the following SQL task in order to understand this use case, the task says count

04:19.290 --> 04:23.890
how many times each customer has made an order with sales greater than 30.

04:23.930 --> 04:26.250
All right, so as usual, we can do it step by step.

04:26.530 --> 04:27.490
So what do we need?

04:27.490 --> 04:28.490
We need the orders.

04:28.490 --> 04:30.170
So let's get the order ID.

04:30.410 --> 04:34.410
And as well let's get the customer ID like this.

04:34.450 --> 04:38.690
And the sales from sales orders.

04:38.730 --> 04:40.210
Let's go and execute it.

04:40.210 --> 04:45.250
So now what else I'm going to do is I'm going to go and order the data by customer ID.

04:45.250 --> 04:47.490
So let's execute it again okay.

04:47.530 --> 04:50.370
So now the task sounds easy but it's a little bit tricky.

04:50.410 --> 04:55.370
We have to count the number of orders for each customer where the sales is higher than 30.

04:55.610 --> 04:58.290
Let's have an example for example this customer number one.

04:58.290 --> 05:01.170
So the total number of orders is three orders right.

05:01.170 --> 05:04.770
But we have to count only the orders where the sales is higher than 30.

05:04.810 --> 05:09.210
And in this example we have only one order where the sales is higher than 30.

05:09.210 --> 05:10.970
So it's only the order number four.

05:10.970 --> 05:14.130
So the count for the customer ID number one should be one.

05:14.130 --> 05:16.610
Now let's check another customer for example that two.

05:16.970 --> 05:22.150
And as you can see we have three orders but none of them have the sales higher than 30.

05:22.150 --> 05:24.310
So the count should be zero here.

05:24.310 --> 05:25.550
So how are we going to do that?

05:25.590 --> 05:30.350
We have to go and flag each row whether it's higher than 30 or not.

05:30.350 --> 05:33.830
So if it's higher than 30 it gets the flag of one.

05:33.870 --> 05:37.430
If it's less than 30 or equal to 30 it's going to get zero.

05:37.470 --> 05:41.910
And then we're going to go and summarize all those flags in order to get the count.

05:41.910 --> 05:43.150
So let's do it step by step.

05:43.150 --> 05:44.710
Let's first create the flag.

05:45.230 --> 05:46.990
So we're going to go and use case.

05:46.990 --> 05:48.830
And then our condition is very easy.

05:48.830 --> 05:49.990
We're going to say when.

05:49.990 --> 05:52.670
So what is the condition sales greater than 30.

05:52.710 --> 05:55.830
So sales is higher than 30.

05:55.910 --> 05:56.990
Then what can happen.

05:56.990 --> 06:00.830
We're going to flag it with the one because later we're going to go and summarize the one.

06:00.870 --> 06:05.070
And now else if it's not higher than 30 equal to 30 or less.

06:05.070 --> 06:06.430
So it's going to get zero.

06:06.470 --> 06:06.870
All right.

06:06.870 --> 06:08.830
So now let's go and end it.

06:08.830 --> 06:12.070
So end let's say sales flag.

06:12.110 --> 06:14.030
Now let's go and execute it and check the results.

06:14.030 --> 06:14.310
All right.

06:14.310 --> 06:20.030
So now if you check the results we got now a very nice flag in order to see which orders has sales higher

06:20.030 --> 06:20.670
than 30.

06:20.710 --> 06:23.350
So now for example let's take the customer ID number one.

06:23.390 --> 06:29.120
As you can see, only the order number four has a sales higher than 30, and it's flagged with one and

06:29.120 --> 06:30.320
all others are zero.

06:30.360 --> 06:32.680
Now let's take that customer ID number three.

06:32.840 --> 06:37.400
And as you can see we have now two orders where the sales is higher than 30.

06:37.440 --> 06:39.480
And as you can see we have the one twice.

06:39.520 --> 06:42.240
And now we can use this flag in order to do the aggregation.

06:42.360 --> 06:47.640
So now if we go and summarize the flag for the customer ID number three, we will get to.

06:47.680 --> 06:51.360
And this is the count of orders where the sales is higher than 30.

06:51.400 --> 06:51.720
Right.

06:51.720 --> 06:52.920
And let's take another example.

06:52.920 --> 06:55.480
The customer ID number two we have everywhere zero.

06:55.480 --> 07:00.600
And if we summarize those values we will get zero which is the count of orders where the sales is higher

07:00.600 --> 07:01.880
than 30 which is correct.

07:01.920 --> 07:06.680
So now as you can see, first we have built an extra column in order to help us doing the aggregation.

07:06.680 --> 07:10.080
And now in the next step we're going to go and aggregate this column.

07:10.080 --> 07:11.120
So let's go and do that.

07:11.160 --> 07:13.200
We don't need all those informations.

07:13.400 --> 07:18.760
The order ID we need the customer ID because it is the granularity for the aggregation.

07:18.760 --> 07:21.120
And let's remove the order by.

07:21.160 --> 07:25.520
And now let's go and group up the data by customer ID.

07:26.080 --> 07:28.580
But of course we need the aggregate function.

07:28.580 --> 07:29.380
So how are we going to do it.

07:29.420 --> 07:32.060
We're going to go and summarize the whole flag.

07:32.220 --> 07:37.260
So now of course we're going to go and rename this since now it is an aggregated column.

07:37.260 --> 07:39.580
So we're going to call it total orders.

07:39.980 --> 07:42.140
So now let's go and execute it.

07:42.180 --> 07:43.420
So now let's go and check the result.

07:43.420 --> 07:45.460
As you can see now we have our four customers.

07:45.660 --> 07:49.580
And for the customer ID number one we got only one order higher than 30.

07:49.620 --> 07:52.740
The second one has no orders higher than 30.

07:52.980 --> 07:54.900
The third we have two and one.

07:54.900 --> 07:56.780
And with that we have solved the task.

07:56.820 --> 08:01.420
Now I would like to add one more thing to our query in order to see the normal aggregations, not the

08:01.420 --> 08:02.780
conditional aggregations.

08:02.780 --> 08:09.460
So usually we go and count for example the star in order to get the total orders.

08:09.460 --> 08:12.460
And let's rename the previous one to high sales.

08:13.220 --> 08:14.940
So let's go and execute it.

08:14.940 --> 08:18.340
So we are just now doing aggregations without any conditions.

08:18.340 --> 08:21.220
And now we can see how many orders did each customer.

08:21.220 --> 08:26.460
So we can see that the customer ID number one did order three times but only one order higher than 30.

08:26.500 --> 08:28.220
So this is a normal aggregation.

08:28.220 --> 08:32.380
And this is a conditional aggregations using the case statements.
